course: dictionary
lesson: 3246
title: The epicentre
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An epicentre is a middle point on the ground. When an earthquake starts, the rock slips deep underground. The epicentre is the point on the surface directly above that spot. «The epicentre is the point on the surface directly above where an earthquake starts.»
explain: Picture a bullseye on a dartboard. The rock slip is hidden far below, but the epicentre is the target on the ground above it. The shaking is usually strongest there, and it spreads outwards in every direction from that point.
explain: You can use epicentre for other things too. Every busy, important place has a centre, and its epicentre is where it is strongest or busiest. A storm can have an epicentre, and so can a party.
fact: An epicentre is the point on the Earth's surface right above where an earthquake starts underground.
fact: «The point underground where the rock slips is the focus, and the point on the surface right above it is the epicentre.»
fact: «Near the epicentre», the shaking of an earthquake is usually strongest.
fact: The epicentre of something can also mean its centre, where it is strongest or busiest, like «the epicentre of the fun».
fact: Spelling: UK writers spell it epicentre, and US writers spell it epicenter. They mean the same thing.
exercise: What is the epicentre of an earthquake? | *The point on the surface directly above where it starts | The loudest sound heard during the shaking | The deep place underground where the rock slips | The crack that appears in the road afterwards
exercise: «The point underground where the rock slips is the focus, and the point on the surface right above it is the epicentre.» What is the focus? | The point on the surface right above it | *The point underground where the rock slips | The highest hill in the area | The town hall next to the river
exercise: Mia says, «The epicentre of the party was the kitchen, where everyone crowded round the food.» What does epicentre mean here? | The front door of the house | The last room people leave | The quietest corner of the house | *The busiest centre, where most of the action is
test: Where is the shaking usually strongest in an earthquake? | At the top of the nearest hill | Only out at sea | Far away from the earthquake | *Near the epicentre
test: «The news said the epicentre of the earthquake was under the town.» What does epicentre mean in this sentence? | The edge of the town | The name of the town | The biggest building in the town | *The point on the surface right above where the earthquake started
exam: Jayden says, «Is epicentre spelt differently in America?» What is the best answer? | No, it is never spelt at all in books | No, it is spelt the same, and it means something different | *Yes, US writers spell it epicenter, but it means the same thing | Yes, and epicenter is a type of rock
